#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run this repo's test suites — the ones a given change should actually run.

.github#860. This repo has 33 test suites and, before this, nothing that ran them. Every suite was
invoked by hand, one at a time, and the only thing that knew WHICH suites a change should run was the
`paths:` filter in each workflow — where a developer's shell cannot reach it. So the local choice was
"type 33 commands" or "push and let CI tell me", and in practice it was the second.

THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT, and it is the whole reason this file is shaped the way it is: DERIVE the
mapping, never COPY it. The change → suite mapping already exists, stated precisely, in the workflows'
`paths:` filters. A selector that RESTATES it becomes a second copy that drifts from the first — this
repo's most-filed bug class (#520, #587/#599, #710, #724 are all "the second copy disagreed"). So this
script parses `.github/workflows/*.yml` and derives the suite set from the filters already there. It
has no table of its own to rot. A workflow that changes its filter changes this script's answer for
free, and a workflow that adds a suite gets it run without anyone editing this file.

WHAT IT DERIVES, per workflow:
  1. Does it gate a PR at all? (`on: pull_request`.) A push-only or `workflow_call`-only workflow
     does not judge your PR, so it does not select suites here.
  2. WHICH changes it gates. `pull_request.paths` is the filter. A `pull_request` trigger with NO
     `paths:` key gates EVERY PR — `coherence.yml` and `closing-keywords.yml` are in that state — so
     their suites are always selected. That distinction is load-bearing and easy to get backwards:
     `pull_request:` with a null value (`coherence.yml`) is "every PR", NOT "no PR trigger".
  3. WHICH suites it runs — scraped from its steps' `run:` blocks, and from any local reusable
     workflow it `uses:`, transitively.

WHY `pull_request` AND NOT `push`. The PR gate is what judges the change you are about to push, so it
is the honest source for "what should I run first". The two filters are duplicated verbatim in most
workflows — and in `adr-coherence.yml` and `skill-registry-coherence.yml` they have already DRIFTED
(the push copy omits patterns the PR copy has), which is precisely why this script reads one of them
rather than trying to reconcile both. That drift is real and filed separately; it does not change what
a PR runs.

HOW IT INVOKES, and the one place it deliberately does NOT derive: WHICH suites to run comes from the
workflows; HOW to run one is canonical per kind (`bash <script>`, `dotnet test <proj>`). The workflows'
literal command lines carry CI-only scaffolding — `--no-build` steps that depend on a prior build,
`--logger`, non-vacuity re-runs that invoke the same project a second time — none of which is a
different SUITE. Normalising by kind is what lets the same project referenced from three workflows
dedupe to one run.

The cost of that line is PRECONDITIONS, and it is worth stating plainly because you will meet it on a
fresh worktree: `coord-engine.yml` builds the engine before running `tests/coord-engine-e2e/` and
`tests/coord-engine-parity/`, and this script does not replicate that build step. Those four suites
fail instantly, saying `the engine must be built first: dotnet build src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli -c Release`.
That is the suite telling you its precondition, not your change breaking. Copying the build step in
here would be copying HOW from the workflows — the very drift this script exists to avoid — and the
suites already state their own remedy, precisely, at the point of failure.

FAILS CLOSED (epic #266). "I could not work out what to run" must never be spelled the same way as
"nothing needed running":

  - A `paths:` pattern using glob syntax this script does not implement, a `paths-ignore:` key, or a
    negated (`!`) pattern is exit 3 — REFUSED, not guessed. Those invert or narrow selection, and a
    selector that silently mis-reads one under-selects: it reports green having run the wrong suites.
  - Discovering zero workflows, or zero suites, or a suite whose script is not on disk, is exit 3.
    A runner that discovers nothing exits 0, and that is the bug this repo keeps re-finding.
  - Selecting zero suites for a real change is a legitimate verdict (nothing gated was touched) and
    is exit 0 — but it SAYS SO, in as many words, rather than printing the same silence as success.

A USEFUL SIDE EFFECT. A selector derived from workflows can only see suites a workflow RUNS — so
anything in `tests/` that no workflow references is either unwired or dead. `tests/merge-guard/` is in
that state today: 69 lines fixturing `.claude/hooks/block-merge-and-main-push.sh`, reachable only by
hand, never once run by CI. `--list` reports that class rather than papering over it.

AND REPORTING IT WAS NOT ENOUGH (.github#2537). `--list` printed the class; nothing ever REFUSED it, so
"unwired" was an unexplained state a suite could drift into in silence — and did.
`tests/review-critic-succession-wire/`, which is itself the gate-inversion proof that critic
succession's refusals can fire, sat invoked by no workflow from the day it landed: the proof that a
gate can say NO had never once been executed. `--assert-wired` turns the report into a verdict, and
`DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED` below is where the two accountable answers — "wired" or "deliberate, because
X" — are the only two answers there are.

Usage:
  scripts/test                 # the suites this change should run — the inner loop
  scripts/test --all           # everything — the pre-push gate
  scripts/test --list          # what would run, and why (which filter matched), without running it
  scripts/test --assert-wired  # every tests/ dir is wired to a workflow, or explained. CI gate.
  scripts/test --base REF      # compare against REF instead of origin/main
  scripts/test --root DIR      # audit a different working tree (used by the fixture)

Exit: 0 = every selected suite passed (or none was selected, said plainly); 1 = a suite failed, or
      `--assert-wired` found an unexplained or stale one; 3 = no verdict, PERMANENT — unreadable
      workflows, unsupported filter syntax, or a discovery that examined nothing.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path

try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    print(
        "scripts/test: PyYAML is required "
        "(python3 -m pip install -r requirements-test.txt)",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    sys.exit(3)

EX_OK, EX_FAIL, EX_NOVERDICT = 0, 1, 3

# `bash tests/x/run.sh` / `dotnet test tests/X.Tests/X.fsproj`, as the workflows spell them.
RX_SHELL = re.compile(r"(?:bash|sh)\s+(tests/[\w.@/-]+\.sh)")
RX_DOTNET = re.compile(r"dotnet\s+test\s+(tests/[\w.@/-]+\.fsproj)")

# Glob syntax we implement. Anything else is refused rather than guessed at.
UNSUPPORTED = re.compile(r"[!\[\]+{}]")

# THE ONLY TWO ACCOUNTABLE ANSWERS (.github#2537). A directory under `tests/` that no workflow runs is
# either WIRED (fix it) or DELIBERATE (say why, here). `--assert-wired` refuses everything else, so
# membership in this dict is a written claim someone signed, not a mute allowlist.
#
# AN ENTRY IS NOT A VERDICT THAT THE STATE IS FINE. It is a verdict that the state is UNDERSTOOD. Four
# of the five below say, in as many words, that they are unwired by OMISSION and are tracked at
# .github#2556 — because the alternative was writing "deliberate" about something that is not, and the
# single property that makes this list worth having is that its entries are true.
#
# HOW IT ROTS, AND WHICH WAY IT FAILS. An entry naming a directory that is now WIRED fails
# (`--assert-wired` calls it STALE): a stale exemption is the dangerous kind, because it would silently
# re-bless that directory if someone later unwired it. An entry naming a directory that is not on disk
# at all is INERT and merely reported — a directory that does not exist cannot be a stray, so the entry
# blesses nothing today. `tests/test-selector/run.sh` is where the repo-specific rule lives that this
# tree carries no inert entries; keeping it there is what lets `--assert-wired` run over a synthetic
# root that has none of these directories.
DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED: dict[str, str] = {
    "tests/delivery-leading-line": (
        "DELIBERATE, and not a suite at all. This directory holds ONE data file — corpus.json, the "
        "single statement of the delivery-marker leading-line boundary (.github#2563) — and no runner, "
        "because a runner here would be a third implementation of the rule its two consumers exist to "
        "agree on. It is not unwired in the sense this gate is built to catch: CI executes every entry "
        "of it TWICE on every pull request, through both consumers' real entry points. "
        "tests/kit-published-coherence/run.sh drives it through "
        "check-kit-published-coherence.py --obligation-arm, and that workflow is deliberately "
        "unfiltered (.github#1597) so it starts on every PR; "
        "tests/FS.GG.Coord.Cli.Tests/DeliveryApplicationTests.fs drives it through "
        "DeliveryApplication.obligationsFromComments under coord-engine.yml, whose paths: filter names "
        "tests/delivery-leading-line/** in BOTH copies precisely so an edit to this file starts it. "
        "Each consumer also asserts the corpus's stated entry count, so this directory cannot go "
        "silently unread the way an unwired suite can."
    ),
    "tests/merge-guard": (
        "DELIBERATE. Fixtures .claude/hooks/block-merge-and-main-push.sh, a LOCAL Claude Code "
        "PreToolUse hook. CI has no agent tool-call to intercept, so there is nothing for a workflow "
        "to exercise. Documented since #860; this entry is that documentation, made load-bearing."
    ),
    "tests/board-paths-live": (
        "UNWIRED BY OMISSION, tracked at .github#2556. Hermetic fixture for "
        "scripts/check-board-paths-live.py. The DETECTOR is wired — board-paths-live.yml runs it "
        "nightly against the live board — but that workflow is schedule/workflow_dispatch only and "
        "invokes the detector directly, never this fixture."
    ),
    "tests/coord-report": (
        "UNWIRED BY OMISSION, tracked at .github#2556, and half-blocked on .github#2555. Fixtures "
        "scripts/fsgg-coord-report, which no workflow runs. Wiring alone would NOT clear this report: "
        "RX_SHELL/RX_DOTNET above are the whole scrape vocabulary, so `python3 tests/coord-report/"
        "run.py` in a workflow is invisible to derivation and the directory would still read UNWIRED."
    ),
    "tests/observed-command-report": (
        "UNWIRED BY OMISSION, tracked at .github#2556. Fixtures its own run-gate-junit.sh, a JUnit "
        "wrapper referenced by nothing outside its own directory — so the remedy is a judgement (wire "
        "it, or retire the wrapper) rather than a missing `run:` line."
    ),
    "tests/release-tag-anchors": (
        "UNWIRED BY OMISSION, tracked at .github#2556. Fixtures scripts/check-release-tag-anchors.py, "
        "which is itself invoked by no workflow — detector and fixture are both unwired, so wiring "
        "this alone would gate a script nothing runs."
    ),
}


class NoVerdict(Exception):
    """Discovery could not produce an answer. Never spelled like 'nothing to run'."""


@dataclass
class Suite:
    """One runnable thing. Identity is its path, so N workflows referencing it dedupe to one run."""

    kind: str  # "shell" | "dotnet"
    path: str

    @property
    def cmd(self) -> list[str]:
        return ["bash", self.path] if self.kind == "shell" else ["dotnet", "test", self.path]

    @property
    def label(self) -> str:
        return " ".join(self.cmd)

    @property
    def tests_dir(self) -> str:
        return "/".join(self.path.split("/")[:2])

    def __hash__(self) -> int:
        return hash(self.path)

    def __eq__(self, other: object) -> bool:
        return isinstance(other, Suite) and self.path == other.path


@dataclass
class Workflow:
    name: str
    gates_prs: bool = False
    # None + gates_prs => a pull_request trigger with no paths: filter => gates EVERY PR.
    # Compiled EAGERLY at discovery, never lazily during selection: a pattern this selector cannot
    # read must be refused whatever mode we are in and wherever it sits in the list. Compiling on
    # first use made the refusal both order-dependent (selection stops at the first MATCH, so a
    # later unreadable pattern was never looked at) and mode-dependent (`--all` never selects, so it
    # never looked at any). A check that only sometimes runs is not a check.
    patterns: list[tuple[str, re.Pattern[str]]] | None = None
    direct: set[Suite] = field(default_factory=set)
    calls: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)  # local `uses: ./.github/workflows/x.yml`

    @property
    def always(self) -> bool:
        return self.gates_prs and self.patterns is None


def translate(pattern: str, where: str) -> re.Pattern[str]:
    """GitHub `paths:` glob -> anchored regex.

    `**` crosses `/`; `*` and `?` do not. Anything outside that vocabulary is REFUSED: a selector
    that silently mis-reads a filter under-selects, and under-selecting reports green having run the
    wrong suites — the failure this script exists to prevent, wearing a different hat.
    """
    if UNSUPPORTED.search(pattern):
        raise NoVerdict(
            f"{where}: paths: pattern {pattern!r} uses glob syntax this selector does not "
            f"implement (one of ! [ ] + {{ }}). Refusing to guess — teach scripts/test the "
            f"syntax rather than let it silently mis-select."
        )
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(pattern):
        c = pattern[i]
        if c == "*":
            if pattern[i : i + 2] == "**":
                out.append(".*")
                i += 2
                continue
            out.append("[^/]*")
        elif c == "?":
            out.append("[^/]?")
        else:
            out.append(re.escape(c))
        i += 1
    return re.compile("".join(out) + r"\Z")


def scrape(node: object, into: set[Suite]) -> None:
    """Pull suite invocations out of a job's steps."""
    for step in node if isinstance(node, list) else []:
        run = str((step or {}).get("run") or "") if isinstance(step, dict) else ""
        for m in RX_SHELL.finditer(run):
            into.add(Suite("shell", m.group(1)))
        for m in RX_DOTNET.finditer(run):
            into.add(Suite("dotnet", m.group(1)))


def discover(root: Path) -> dict[str, Workflow]:
    wf_dir = root / ".github" / "workflows"
    files = sorted(wf_dir.glob("*.yml")) + sorted(wf_dir.glob("*.yaml"))
    if not files:
        raise NoVerdict(f"no workflows under {wf_dir} — examining nothing is not a clean audit (#266)")

    out: dict[str, Workflow] = {}
    for f in files:
        try:
            doc = yaml.safe_load(f.read_text()) or {}
        except yaml.YAMLError as e:
            raise NoVerdict(f"{f.name}: will not parse ({e})") from e
        if not isinstance(doc, dict):
            raise NoVerdict(f"{f.name}: not a mapping")

        # `on:` is YAML 1.1 boolean true. Both spellings, because either may reach us.
        on = doc.get(True, doc.get("on"))
        wf = Workflow(name=f.name)

        # NORMALISE the three legal spellings of `on:` before reading it. `on: pull_request` and
        # `on: [push, pull_request]` are as valid as the mapping form, and both mean "every PR, no
        # filter". Reading only the mapping form did not refuse them — it silently decided the
        # workflow gated NOTHING and dropped its suites, which is under-selection: the one failure
        # mode this script exists to prevent, arrived at by being fussy about globs and credulous
        # about triggers. Anything that is none of the three is refused, not guessed.
        if isinstance(on, str):
            on = {on: None}
        elif isinstance(on, list):
            on = {str(k): None for k in on}
        if not isinstance(on, dict):
            raise NoVerdict(
                f"{f.name}: `on:` is {type(on).__name__}, not a string, list, or mapping — this "
                f"selector cannot tell what triggers the workflow, and guessing would silently "
                f"under-select (#266)."
            )

        if "pull_request" in on:
            wf.gates_prs = True
            pr = on["pull_request"]
            # Scoped to pull_request, because that is the only trigger this selector derives from.
            # A `push: {paths-ignore:}` cannot change what a PR runs, and refusing the whole run
            # over it would be a false alarm that breaks the tool for everyone.
            if isinstance(pr, dict) and "paths-ignore" in pr:
                raise NoVerdict(
                    f"{f.name}: pull_request uses paths-ignore:, which INVERTS selection. This "
                    f"selector only implements paths:. Refusing to guess (#266)."
                )
            # `pull_request:` (null), or one carrying only `types:`, has no paths filter and so
            # gates every PR. Only an explicit `paths:` narrows it.
            if isinstance(pr, dict) and "paths" in pr:
                pats = [str(p) for p in (pr.get("paths") or [])]
                if not pats:
                    raise NoVerdict(f"{f.name}: pull_request.paths: is present but empty")
                wf.patterns = [(p, translate(p, f.name)) for p in pats]

        for job in (doc.get("jobs") or {}).values():
            if not isinstance(job, dict):
                continue
            scrape(job.get("steps"), wf.direct)
            uses = job.get("uses")
            if isinstance(uses, str) and uses.startswith("./"):
                wf.calls.append(uses.split("/")[-1])

        out[f.name] = wf
    return out


def suites_of(wf: Workflow, all_wfs: dict[str, Workflow], seen: set[str] | None = None) -> set[Suite]:
    """A workflow's suites, following local `uses:` edges so a reusable callee's suites count."""
    seen = seen or set()
    if wf.name in seen:
        return set()
    seen.add(wf.name)
    out = set(wf.direct)
    for callee in wf.calls:
        if callee in all_wfs:
            out |= suites_of(all_wfs[callee], all_wfs, seen)
    return out


def git(root: Path, *args: str) -> str:
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), *args], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        raise NoVerdict(f"git {' '.join(args)}: {p.stderr.strip()}")
    return p.stdout


def changed_files(root: Path, base: str) -> list[str]:
    """Everything this branch changed vs base, plus the working tree — the inner loop's real input."""
    rev = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", base],
        capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if rev.returncode != 0:
        raise NoVerdict(
            f"base {base!r} does not resolve. Pass --base with a ref that exists "
            f"(e.g. --base HEAD to select on the working tree alone)."
        )
    mb = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(root), "merge-base", base, "HEAD"], capture_output=True, text=True
    )
    anchor = mb.stdout.strip() if mb.returncode == 0 and mb.stdout.strip() else base

    files: set[str] = set()
    files |= set(git(root, "diff", "--name-only", anchor, "HEAD").split())
    files |= set(git(root, "diff", "--name-only", "HEAD").split())  # staged + unstaged
    files |= set(git(root, "ls-files", "--others", "--exclude-standard").split())  # untracked
    return sorted(f for f in files if f)


@dataclass
class Reason:
    workflow: str
    pattern: str | None  # None => the workflow has no paths: filter; it gates every PR
    example: str | None


def select(
    wfs: dict[str, Workflow], changed: list[str]
) -> dict[Suite, list[Reason]]:
    picked: dict[Suite, list[Reason]] = {}
    for wf in wfs.values():
        if not wf.gates_prs:
            continue
        reason: Reason | None = None
        if wf.always:
            reason = Reason(wf.name, None, None)
        else:
            for pat, rx in wf.patterns or []:
                hit = next((f for f in changed if rx.match(f)), None)
                if hit:
                    reason = Reason(wf.name, pat, hit)
                    break
        if reason is None:
            continue
        for s in suites_of(wf, wfs):
            picked.setdefault(s, []).append(reason)
    return picked


def every_suite(wfs: dict[str, Workflow]) -> set[Suite]:
    out: set[Suite] = set()
    for wf in wfs.values():
        out |= suites_of(wf, wfs)
    return out


def unwired(root: Path, known: set[Suite]) -> list[str]:
    """tests/ dirs no workflow references — either unwired or dead. Reported, never papered over."""
    tests = root / "tests"
    if not tests.is_dir():
        return []
    wired = {s.tests_dir for s in known}
    return sorted(
        f"tests/{d.name}"
        for d in tests.iterdir()
        if d.is_dir() and f"tests/{d.name}" not in wired
    )


def assert_wired(root: Path, known: set[Suite]) -> int:
    """`unwired()` as a VERDICT rather than a note (.github#2537).

    The report existed from #860 and nothing ever depended on it, so "unwired" was a state work could
    drift into in silence — and `tests/review-critic-succession-wire/`, the gate-inversion proof that
    critic succession's refusals can fire, drifted into it on the day it landed. Reporting a class you
    never refuse is how the class survives being known about.
    """
    tests = root / "tests"
    present = sorted(f"tests/{d.name}" for d in tests.iterdir() if d.is_dir()) if tests.is_dir() else []
    if not present:
        # THE VACUITY TRAP (#266, and .github#2534 measured it live: a gate that scanned an empty
        # corpus passed green and was caught only by a separate non-vacuity leg). Every set below is
        # empty over an empty `tests/`, so this assertion would be its most confident when it had
        # examined nothing at all. Refuse instead — "I found no suites" must never be spelled the
        # same way as "every suite is wired".
        raise NoVerdict(
            f"no test directories under {tests} — every set this assertion compares is empty, so it "
            f"would pass having examined nothing, which is the shape of failure it exists to catch"
        )

    stray = set(unwired(root, known))
    explained = sorted(stray & set(DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED))
    unexplained = sorted(stray - set(DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED))
    # Exempted, but not a stray: either it got wired (STALE — it would re-bless the directory if
    # someone unwired it again) or it is not on disk (INERT — it blesses nothing today).
    stale = sorted(d for d in DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED if d not in stray and d in present)
    inert = sorted(d for d in DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED if d not in stray and d not in present)

    print(
        f"scripts/test --assert-wired: {len(present)} director(ies) under tests/, "
        f"{len(present) - len(stray)} wired, {len(explained)} explained, "
        f"{len(unexplained)} unexplained, {len(stale)} stale"
    )
    for d in explained:
        print(f"  explained  {d}/ — {DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED[d]}")
    for d in inert:
        print(f"  INERT      {d}/ — exempted here, but no such directory is on disk")

    rc = EX_OK
    for d in unexplained:
        print(f"  UNWIRED    {d}/ — no workflow invokes it, and DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED does not explain it")
        rc = EX_FAIL
    for d in stale:
        print(f"  STALE      {d}/ — exempted here, but a workflow runs it now; delete the entry")
        rc = EX_FAIL

    if rc != EX_OK:
        print(
            "\nscripts/test: a suite no workflow runs has never been run by CI (#860), and a suite CI "
            "has never run\nis a proof nobody has ever observed fire (.github#2537). Wire it in a "
            "workflow's `run:` step, or\nrecord why it is deliberate in DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED "
            "(scripts/test). Those are the two answers."
        )
    return rc


def check_on_disk(root: Path, suites: set[Suite]) -> None:
    """A workflow naming a suite that is not there means discovery is wrong. Exit 3, never 0."""
    missing = sorted(s.path for s in suites if not (root / s.path).exists())
    if missing:
        raise NoVerdict(
            "a workflow invokes suites that are not on disk — discovery is unreliable, so no "
            "verdict: " + ", ".join(missing)
        )


def run(root: Path, suites: list[Suite]) -> int:
    started = time.monotonic()
    results: list[tuple[Suite, int, float]] = []
    for i, s in enumerate(suites, 1):
        print(f"\n\033[1m── [{i}/{len(suites)}] {s.label}\033[0m", flush=True)
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        rc = subprocess.run(s.cmd, cwd=str(root)).returncode
        results.append((s, rc, time.monotonic() - t0))

    failed = [(s, rc) for s, rc, _ in results if rc != 0]
    print(f"\n{'─' * 72}")
    for s, rc, dt in results:
        print(f"  {'PASS' if rc == 0 else f'FAIL (exit {rc})':<16} {dt:6.1f}s  {s.label}")
    print(
        f"\n{len(results)} suite(s): {len(results) - len(failed)} passed, {len(failed)} failed "
        f"— {time.monotonic() - started:.0f}s"
    )
    if failed:
        # Report the exit code, not the suite's own summary line: a suite that prints a green
        # summary and exits non-zero is a real, unpinned flake in this repo (#860), and the exit
        # code is the half that is load-bearing.
        print("\nFAILED:")
        for s, rc in failed:
            print(f"  exit {rc}  {s.label}")
        return EX_FAIL
    return EX_OK


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True, description="Run the suites this change should run.")
    ap.add_argument("--all", action="store_true", help="run every suite, not just the selected ones")
    ap.add_argument("--list", action="store_true", help="print what would run, and why; run nothing")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--assert-wired",
        action="store_true",
        help="every tests/ dir is wired to a workflow, or explained in DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED; run nothing",
    )
    ap.add_argument("--base", default=os.environ.get("FSGG_TEST_BASE", "origin/main"))
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=None, help="working tree to audit (default: this repo)")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    root = Path(args.root).resolve() if args.root else Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent

    changed: list[str] = []
    try:
        wfs = discover(root)
        known = every_suite(wfs)
        if not known:
            raise NoVerdict(
                "no workflow invokes any test suite — a selector that discovers nothing would "
                "report green over an untested tree (#266)"
            )
        check_on_disk(root, known)

        # Before selection: this mode judges the WIRING, not a change, so it needs no diff and no base.
        if args.assert_wired:
            return assert_wired(root, known)

        if args.all:
            chosen = {s: [Reason("--all", None, None)] for s in known}
        else:
            changed = changed_files(root, args.base)
            chosen = select(wfs, changed)
    except NoVerdict as e:
        print(f"\033[31mscripts/test: no verdict\033[0m — {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return EX_NOVERDICT

    ordered = sorted(chosen, key=lambda s: (s.kind, s.path))
    stray = unwired(root, known)

    if args.list:
        if args.all:
            print(f"\033[1m--all — every suite ({len(ordered)})\033[0m")
            for s in ordered:
                print(f"  {s.label}")
        else:
            print(
                f"\033[1m{len(ordered)} suite(s) selected\033[0m — {len(changed)} changed file(s) "
                f"vs {args.base}"
            )
            for s in ordered:
                print(f"\n  {s.label}")
                for r in chosen[s]:
                    if r.pattern is None:
                        print(f"      {r.workflow}: no paths: filter — gates every PR")
                    else:
                        print(f"      {r.workflow}: {r.pattern}  ←  {r.example}")
        if stray:
            print(
                f"\n\033[33mUNWIRED\033[0m — in tests/, invoked by no workflow, so CI has never "
                f"run it (#860):"
            )
            for d in stray:
                # Annotated, because "unwired" alone reads as one state and is two (.github#2537):
                # a deliberate one somebody signed, and an unexplained one `--assert-wired` refuses.
                note = "explained" if d in DELIBERATELY_UNWIRED else "UNEXPLAINED — --assert-wired fails"
                print(f"  {d}/  [{note}]")
        return EX_OK

    if not ordered:
        # NOT the same as success, and it must never be spelled the same way (#266).
        print(
            f"scripts/test: \033[1mno suite is gated by this change\033[0m — 0 of "
            f"{len(known)} selected, vs {args.base}. Nothing to run.\n"
            f"  `scripts/test --list` shows why; `scripts/test --all` runs everything."
        )
        return EX_OK

    print(f"scripts/test: {len(ordered)} of {len(known)} suite(s), vs {args.base}")
    rc = run(root, ordered)
    if stray and rc == EX_OK:
        print(f"\nnote: {len(stray)} unwired suite(s) no workflow runs — scripts/test --list")
    return rc


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
